ZERO OS development plan

This is a plan, not a list of completed features. A feature is complete only
when its source is integrated, the system builds, and relevant tests pass.
All guest implementation and the compiler remain original Zero code.
External specifications describe compatibility formats, not code to copy.

Established storage foundation
1. Bounded, validated read/create/update/delete for ext4, NTFS and Btrfs.
2. Independent volume roots A:| through Z:|, GO and disk inventory.
3. Partition discovery with strict bounds, integrity checks and no writes.
4. BIOS and UEFI boot of the larger kernel on x86-32 and x86-64.
5. Cross-volume isolation, persistent-write and filesystem-integrity tests.

Next storage work
6. Directories, GO into directories, per-volume current directories.
7. Binary files and streaming reads/writes beyond the current400-byte limit.
8. Rename, copy, move, file information and available-space reporting.
9. Larger ext4 profiles, journal replay and checksummed metadata support.
10. NTFS cluster allocation, nonresident data and directory index splitting.
11. Larger Btrfs trees, snapshots, data checksums and further chunk profiles.
12. Recovery tools, transaction fault injection and power-loss tests.
13. Explicit disk/partition creation tools with preview of affected sectors.
14. Persistent volume identifiers and saved letter assignments.
15. AHCI, NVMe and USB storage, followed by removable-device handling.

Language and compiler
Priority: Zero capabilities and reusable system functions; Ezero convenience.
16. Named Zero procedures, structured records and explicit module interfaces.
17. Source-position diagnostics and readable compile errors, especially in
    the beginner Ezero workflow; multiline editing and file-based execution.
18. Persistent source/project compilation inside Zero OS. Native Zero/Ezero
    compiler execution via memory streams is implemented; file/process APIs
    and protected application execution remain further work.
19. In-system editor, reproducible builds and documented bootstrap provenance.
20. Allocation bounds checking and improved runtime fault reports.

Kernel and applications
21. Interrupt-driven timer/input and preemptive scheduling.
22. Protected user processes, virtual memory, exceptions and process cleanup.
23. Executable loading, arguments, exit statuses and task control.
24. Synchronization primitives, asynchronous disk queues and later multicore.
25. Mouse input, arbitrary GUI applications, text editing, selectable fonts
    and clipboard. Keyboard terminal windows are implemented in0.8.0.
26. Double buffering and a retained graphics API for smooth animation.
27. Original image decoder, audio mixer and basic media applications.
28. Clock/calendar, power management and clean shutdown/restart.
29. Broader network adapters, physical WiFi drivers, TLS and richer protocols.
    PCnet automatic DHCP, DNS and bounded TCP/HTTP are implemented.
30. Users, permissions, application capabilities and update verification.

Acceptance rules
Test ordinary use, malformed input, resource exhaustion and restart behavior.
Filesystem writes additionally require independent format-integrity checks.
Keep tests on private image copies and preserve existing user data disks.
Reject unsupported features explicitly instead of guessing on disk formats.
Record tested VM/firmware/CPU combinations; do not imply untested hardware.
Revisit this ordering as prerequisites and actual failures become known.

Current0.11 follow-through
Implemented: native Zero/Ezero host file/argument/memory APIs, standalone
compiler CLIs, keyboard application menu/system/storage/network, dedicated
host-directory preparation, original SHA256 and local staged-release rollback.
Added: UEFI x64 sibling-ISO boot on firmware-readable volumes, v2 deployment,
Zero filesync/hostwait, local periodic update checking and non-destructive
recovery into a new store, Ezero lines/join text conveniences.
Still required for true Wubi: early NTFS/ext4/Btrfs host filesystem loading,
host-file-backed block device, boot-menu registration and transactional rollback.
Still required for rolling automatic updates: installed A/B boot state,
verified startup selection/fallback, signed release manifests/trusted keys,
configured authenticated transport, startup scheduling and durable directory
metadata publication. Native file flushing and process-local waits now exist.
Physical WiFi connection still needs original chipset drivers and security.
These are unfinished implementations, not advertised completed features.
